"fluoxetine","id":"2","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"dopamine agonist","id":"3","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"apomorphine","id":"4","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"choreic mid-dose dyskinesias","id":"1","label":"Disease"},{"text":"parkinsonian motor disability","id":"2","label":"Disease"}],"gold_relations":"apomorphine | CID | dyskinesias\nfluoxetine | CID | dyskinesias","labels":"Chemical,Disease","metrics":{"precision":0.3333333333333333,"recall":0.5,"f1":0.4,"tp":1,"fp":2,"fn":1,"rel_precision":0.3333333333333333,"rel_recall":0.5,"rel_f1":0.4,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":2,"rel_fn":1},"pairs":[{"head":"levodopa-responsive","tail":"choreic mid-dose dyskinesias","head_id":"1","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"levodopa-responsive","tail":"parkinsonian motor disability","head_id":"1","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"fluoxetine","tail":"choreic mid-dose dyskinesias","head_id":"2","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"fluoxetine","tail":"parkinsonian motor disability","head_id":"2","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"dopamine agonist","tail":"choreic mid-dose dyskinesias","head_id":"3","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"dopamine agonist","tail":"parkinsonian motor disability","head_id":"3","tail_id":"2"},{"head":"apomorphine","tail":"choreic mid-dose dyskinesias","head_id":"4","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"apomorphine","tail":"parkinsonian motor disability","head_id":"4","tail_id":"2"}],"relations":["1 | 1","3 | 1","4 | 1"],"text":"Levodopa-induced dyskinesias are improved by fluoxetine. We evaluated the severity of motor disability and dyskinesias in seven levodopa-responsive patients with Parkinson's disease after an acute challenge with the mixed dopamine agonist, apomorphine, before and after the administration of fluoxetine (20 mg twice per day) for 11 +/- 1 days. After fluoxetine treatment, there was a significant 47% improvement (p < 0.05) of apomorphine-induced dyskinesias without modification of parkinsonian motor disability. The dyskinesias were reduced predominantly in the lower limbs during the onset and disappearance of dystonic dyskinesias (onset- and end-of-dose dyskinesias) and in the upper limbs during choreic mid-dose dyskinesias. The results suggest that increased brain serotoninergic transmission with fluoxetine may reduce levodopa- or dopamine agonist-induced dyskinesias without aggravating parkinsonian motor disability."}